    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d803">

                <pb facs="0881" n="595" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg595"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.711/266</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-02-21T19:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 21, 1916, 7 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 11 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3832. Your 2904, February 19. Sir Edward Grey informs me that the British answer
                    to your protest of January 4 against removal and detention of mails is ready and
                    be delivered as soon as the French answer is received, which he expects now any
                    day. I did not mention Van Dyke’s informal notice because an earlier private
                    conversation with an official of the Foreign Office had brought out the fact
                    that the British authorities in the United Kingdom are not warranted in
                    receiving communications from our representatives to other governments. The <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Rotterdam</hi> incident of January 16 will be covered by
                    the forthcoming note.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
